1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,390
Your budget: a million evaluations to average a function of a hundred inputs.

2
00:00:05,640 --> 00:00:08,920
Spread them out as an even grid; that feels thorough.

3
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:11,360
How many values does each input get?

4
00:00:12,966 --> 00:00:13,616
One.

5
00:00:13,816 --> 00:00:16,906
The whole grid is one point, the centre of the box.

6
00:00:17,106 --> 00:00:20,616
Two values per input would take two to the hundredth points.

7
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:25,470
Pricing an option on a hundred stocks is this kind of average.

8
00:00:25,670 --> 00:00:33,960
Since Metropolis and Ulam's 1949 paper, such averages are usually done with random points, like this one.

9
00:00:34,210 --> 00:00:36,480
How far off would a million of them land?

10
00:00:36,630 --> 00:00:37,800
Hold that thought.

11
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:40,620
Start with two inputs: a square.

12
00:00:40,820 --> 00:00:48,980
Our function is one inside this quarter disc, zero outside, so its average is the disc's share: exactly one half.

13
00:00:49,066 --> 00:00:54,116
A ten by ten grid puts fifty-two of its hundred points inside: two points off.

14
00:00:54,233 --> 00:00:58,413
A hundred random darts put forty-four inside: six points off.

15
00:00:58,663 --> 00:01:00,883
In two dimensions, the grid wins.

16
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:04,530
Now give each a million points, and add inputs.

17
00:01:04,730 --> 00:01:08,760
The disc becomes a ball that always covers exactly half the box.

18
00:01:08,866 --> 00:01:17,486
The grid splits its million across the axes: a thousand values per axis in two dimensions, then a hundred, ten, three.

19
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:22,360
From twenty dimensions on, just one: the centre, which sits inside the ball.

20
00:01:22,510 --> 00:01:26,060
So the grid says the ball fills the box: fifty points off.

21
00:01:26,166 --> 00:01:32,256
Even two values on nineteen of the axes, half a million points, is eighteen points off.

22
00:01:32,506 --> 00:01:35,936
The darts stay under a tenth of a point in every dimension.

23
00:01:36,533 --> 00:01:43,193
Look at the shadows on one axis: the grid's hundred points cast ten, a hundred darts cast a hundred.

24
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:45,200
With N points over d axes,

25
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:47,840
each axis gets the d-th root of N.

26
00:01:48,433 --> 00:01:50,543
But shadows alone are not the fix.

27
00:01:50,693 --> 00:01:58,963
A million points on the diagonal, each one a flat line here, give every axis a million values, and still miss by eight points.

28
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:08,340
Darts win another way: each one lands inside with a chance equal to the answer, on its own, in two dimensions or a hundred.

29
00:02:08,540 --> 00:02:11,790
Their estimate is a share of heads in N coin flips.

30
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:13,650
One flip is zero or one.

31
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:18,540
Its average is a half, and every flip misses that by exactly a half.

32
00:02:18,633 --> 00:02:21,963
Repeat the hundred-dart experiment three hundred times.

33
00:02:22,163 --> 00:02:27,173
In two dimensions the estimates spread this wide; in a hundred, just as wide.

34
00:02:27,423 --> 00:02:30,493
Quadruple the darts, and both get twice as narrow.

35
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:39,520
Each dart misses by a half, up or down at random, so the misses partly cancel: sixteen darts stray about four halves, not sixteen.

36
00:02:39,770 --> 00:02:45,130
N darts stray about root N halves; per dart, that's a half over the square root of N.

37
00:02:46,033 --> 00:02:49,733
Any other function swaps that half for its own spread, sigma.

38
00:02:50,333 --> 00:02:52,843
The root N stays, and there's no d in it.

39
00:02:53,043 --> 00:02:57,283
A grid's error, even for smooth functions, has d in its exponent.

40
00:02:58,866 --> 00:03:05,316
So, back to our ball, with a million darts, in any dimension: how far off is a typical estimate?

41
00:03:06,816 --> 00:03:10,176
One half over a thousand: five hundredths of a point.

42
00:03:10,976 --> 00:03:14,716
Two runs in three land closer; all six of ours did.

43
00:03:15,333 --> 00:03:23,963
In 2012, Bergstra and Bengio found that random search tuned neural networks as well as grid search, in a fraction of the time.

44
00:03:24,163 --> 00:03:31,383
If one setting matters most, nine grid trials test three values of it; nine random trials test nine.

45
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:35,010
Darts do have a blind spot: tiny answers.

46
00:03:35,210 --> 00:03:41,030
By thirty dimensions, a radius-one ball fills two parts in a hundred trillion of the box.

47
00:03:41,230 --> 00:03:48,550
Below this line, a million darts expect under one hit, so they usually report zero: off by the whole answer.

48
00:03:49,666 --> 00:03:51,776
The darts never noticed the axes:

49
00:03:52,076 --> 00:03:54,656
a million coin flips, in any dimension.

